<div id="features" class="features">
  <section class="feature" aria-labelledby="capture-title">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2 id="capture-title">Highlight where you already read.</h2>
      <p>Twine is natively integrated with Safari. Highlight any text on any webpage and recall it later.</p>
      <ul class="checks" role="list">
        <li><span><strong>On Mac:</strong> select text and press <kbd>⌃⇧H</kbd>. Or turn on auto-highlight to save selections as you read.</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>On iPhone:</strong> select text and tap Twine in Safari's page menu.</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>Still there when you come back:</strong> revisit a page and your highlights reappear, as long as the text and surrounding context still match.</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="media">
      {% shot "capture", "mac", "The training section of Wikipedia's large language model article in Safari, with the passage about model training costs highlighted in yellow.", "Safari on a public article with one or two saved Twine highlights. No transient messages or recording needed.", false, "Mac · A highlighted passage in Safari", 1960, 1280 %}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature feature-library" aria-labelledby="library-title">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2 id="library-title">Remember three words. Find the whole passage.</h2>
      <p>Every highlight goes into one library on your Mac and iPhone. Search for the words you remember, browse by website and page, or filter to today, the past week, or the past month.</p>
      <ul class="checks" role="list">
        <li><span><strong>Search everything</strong> in your library</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>Jump back to the source</strong> in one click</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>Find highlights in Spotlight</strong> and use them in Shortcuts</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>Recent highlights in the menu bar</strong> on Mac</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>All of this is free.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="media">
      {% shot "library-duo", "duo", "Twine on iPhone Duo with Wikipedia highlights on the left and the selected training-cost passage, source and actions on the right.", "iPhone Duo open in landscape orientation, with the library and selected highlight details visible side by side.", false, "iPhone Duo · Wikipedia highlights and the selected passage", 2000, 1407 %}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature feature-ai" aria-labelledby="ai-title">
    <div class="copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Twine Pro</p>
      <h2 id="ai-title">Give your AI tools your reading — through a local, read-only connection.</h2>
      <p>Your AI assistant knows the internet. It doesn't know what <em>you</em> found worth keeping. AI Access lets a compatible AI tool on your Mac search and read your highlights, each with its source link, so its answers can draw on what you've actually read.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="media media-settings">
      {% shot "ai-access", "window", "Twine's AI settings on a Mac with AI Access enabled, a local server URL and controls to copy connection settings or regenerate the token; no token value is displayed.", "Twine Settings > AI > AI Access, with the local server enabled and connection controls visible. Keep access tokens concealed; no chat response needed.", false, "Mac · Read-only access for connected AI tools", 1040, 1436 %}
    </div>
    <div class="ai-details">
      <ul class="prompts" role="list" aria-label="Example requests">
        <li>“What have I highlighted about pricing strategy? Cite the sources.”</li>
        <li>“Summarize what I saved this week.”</li>
        <li>“Find the passage I highlighted about sleep and memory, and link me to it.”</li>
        <li>“Draft an outline from my highlights on urban planning.”</li>
      </ul>
      <ul class="checks" role="list">
        <li><span><strong>Local.</strong> Twine runs the connection on your Mac. Your library isn't uploaded to a developer-run server.</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>Read-only.</strong> AI tools can search and read. They cannot add, edit, or delete through this connection.</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>Off by default.</strong> Nothing is shared through AI Access until you turn it on.</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>Yours to revoke.</strong> Access uses a token you can replace at any time.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note">Works with compatible AI tools on your Mac that support local MCP servers over HTTP with a bearer token. Cloud-hosted chat services can't reach it. Twine must be running. AI tools and their provider fees are separate. A connected tool handles what it reads under its own policy; these are example requests, not guaranteed answers.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="feature" aria-labelledby="context-title">
    <div class="copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Twine Pro</p>
      <h2 id="context-title">Remember why you saved it.</h2>
      <p>A highlight out of context is easy to misread six months later. With Pro, Twine can add a short note explaining how each passage fits the text around it. The note is written on your device by Apple Intelligence, saved with the highlight, and synced through your iCloud.</p>
      <p class="note">Off by default. Requires Apple Intelligence and a supported language. Context notes are not shared with AI Access tools.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="media">
      {% shot "context", "phone", "An iPhone showing the highlighted Wikipedia training-cost passage, its Background explanation, the Refresh control and the source.", "An iPhone highlight detail with a completed AI context explanation. Use a public passage with enough surrounding text; avoid loading or unavailable states.", false, "iPhone · The passage and its on-device explanation", 1206, 2622 %}
    </div>
  </section>
</div>

<section class="band" aria-labelledby="privacy-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 id="privacy-title">Twine doesn't send us your library. We built it that way.</h2>
    <div>
      <p>What you read and what you choose to keep says a lot about you. Twine has no account system and no developer-run library server. Your highlights live on your own devices and in your private iCloud.</p>
      <ul class="checks" role="list">
        <li><span><strong>No account.</strong> Install and start highlighting.</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>No developer library server.</strong> Sync runs through your private iCloud database.</span></li>
        <li>No ads, trackers, or developer-run analytics.</li>
        <li><span><strong>AI on your terms.</strong> AI Access is local, read-only, and off until you switch it on.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note">Highlighted websites receive display-name and icon requests. Optional Apple diagnostics, support messages and public GitHub feedback are covered in the privacy policy. A connected AI tool handles what it reads under its own policy.</p>
      <p class="more"><a class="text-link" href="/privacy">Read the privacy policy</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

